README-ERRMSG -- the error message file, and BASIC09's numbers==============================================================SYS/errmsg is the text `perr` and the shell print for an error number.  Itsformat is fixed and has no comment syntax: a line is either    NNN:MMM text            an entry, class and code     text                   a continuation of the entry above itand nothing else.  This note exists because that leaves nowhere in the fileitself to say the following.PROVENANCE.  The file here is clean-room.  The code numbers and their E$names are standard OS-9 facts; every description is original prose writtenfor this collection from its own error-code reference, checked against theOS-9 v2.4 Technical Reference Manual.  None of it is copied from Microware'serrmsg, which is not here.BASIC09 NUMBERS ITS OWN ERRORS 10-80.  runb reports them in the same000:NNN form the kernel uses, so both schemes share class 000.  They aredifferent numbering systems that happen to share a channel: 43 is BASIC09's"Unknown procedure", while OS-9 has no 43 at all.  Codes above 80 are OS-9'salone, and 1-9 are unused by both.They collide at exactly four codes -- 64, 65, 66 and 67 -- where OS-9 hasmath-library errors.  Those four entries give the OS-9 meaning first and theBASIC09 reading after it, because one key cannot resolve to one meaning whentwo systems share it.WHY YOU MAY NOT SEE THIS TEXT.  Under os9exec the emulator prints errornames and descriptions from a table compiled into itself, not from thisfile.  Substituting a different errmsg changes nothing there -- verified byrunning perr against two different files and getting identical output.  Onreal OS-9 the file is what `perr` reads.The two BASIC09 programs on this disk are bio and blackjack.See DOC/INDEX for how to run them.